---
/**
 * The landing page's migratr section: what migratr does, in claims taken from
 * its README and its 0.2.0 changelog, its installer, and the way to its docs.
 * Rafters components and props only, at the designer's sizes.
 *
 * @cognitive-load 3/10 - Five short claims, one command, one link
 * @accessibility Headings in order; the docs link is named by its text
 */
import Container from "@/components/ui/container.astro";
import { H2, P } from "@/components/ui/typography.astro";
import { migratrRelease } from "@/src/migratr/release";
import Install from "./install.astro";
---

<Container gap="16">
  <Container gap="6">
    <H2>migratr</H2>
    <P>
      migratr changes the shape of a SQLite database; smugglr moves its rows. A migration is a JSON file, and a ledger
      table in the database records which have been applied.
    </P>
    <P>
      plan prints the statements a run would execute and the snapshot each destructive step would take, and changes
      nothing. A destructive step takes its snapshot first, and restore puts the database back.
    </P>
    <P>down reverses a migration only when every operation in it has an inverse.</P>
    <P>
      A table rebuild uses no TEMP objects and leaves foreign keys on, so the same rebuild runs on a local file and on a
      D1 database.
    </P>
    <P>
      With smugglr sync --migratr, a migration runs on the local database first and stops for you to check it; the next
      run carries it to the place.
    </P>
    <Container as="article">
      <p><a href="/migratr/">The migratr docs</a></p>
    </Container>
  </Container>
  <Install release={migratrRelease} />
</Container>
